# Discrete colour scales: scale_colour_discrete

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

mpg = Giggly.Datasets.mpg()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`scale_colour_discrete()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/scale_colour_discrete.html),
which covers `scale_fill_discrete()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows
ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code
and names what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

`scale_colour_discrete()` and `scale_fill_discrete()` are the scales ggplot2 uses by default when a
column of discrete values is mapped to `colour` or `fill`, giving each value its own colour from a
palette. Their default palette is a setting of the theme. Giggly draws a discrete column mapped to
`colour` or `fill` as `scale_colour_discrete()` does by default, in hues evenly spaced round the
colour wheel with a legend, and has `Giggly.Plot.scale_color_discrete/2` and
`Giggly.Plot.scale_fill_discrete/2`, whose `palette:` is a palette's name, as the scales package
names them, or a list of colours, and takes no palette from the theme (`palette.colour.discrete`).
A plot is described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with `Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG
document. The setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells write `Plot.new/2`, and defines
`render`, which draws a plot and shows it below the cell with `Kino.Image`. It binds ggplot2's
`mpg`, `Giggly.Datasets.mpg/0`, to `mpg`.

## A palette given to the scale

`scales::pal_brewer()` is a palette from the scales package, here ColorBrewer's `Dark2`.

```r
# A standard plot
p <- ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy, colour = class)) +
  geom_point()

# You can use the scale to give a palette directly
p + scale_colour_discrete(palette = scales::pal_brewer(palette = "Dark2"))
```

Giggly names the palette, `"Dark2"`, where ggplot2 is handed the palette `scales::pal_brewer()`
makes; ggplot2 looks a name up as the same palette.

```elixir
p =
  mpg
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :displ, y: :hwy, colour: :class})
  |> Plot.geom_point()

p
|> Plot.scale_color_discrete(palette: "Dark2")
|> render.()
```

## A palette from the theme

```r
# The default colours are encoded into the theme
p + theme(palette.colour.discrete = scales::pal_grey())
```

Not in Giggly yet: the theme's `palette.colour.discrete` setting.

## A default for every plot

`update_theme()` changes the theme every later plot is drawn in, and returns the one it replaced.
Giggly has no global settings: every option is an argument.

```r
# You can globally set default colour palette via the theme
old <- update_theme(palette.colour.discrete = scales::pal_viridis())

# Plot now shows new global default
p
```

Not in Giggly yet: the theme's `palette.colour.discrete` setting.

## Restoring the theme

```r
# Restoring the previous theme
theme_set(old)
```

`theme_set()` makes the theme saved in `old` the default again, undoing `update_theme()`. It draws
nothing. Giggly has no global settings, so there is nothing to restore.
